      "claim": "L3 oral-exposure records for quaternary ammonium antimuscarinics trospium and glycopyrronium show pg/mL-level Cmax and low AUC after oral doses (trospium Cmax 1517–2717 pg/mL, AUC24 ~14,900–28,590 pg·h/mL; glycopyrronium Cmax 318 pg/mL after 2 mg), consistent with low oral systemic exposure.",
      "card_ids": ["C74", "C104", "C61", "C71", "C84"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4 shows trospium has high aqueous solubility (>100 mg/mL), so the low oral exposure in this class is not solubility-limited; the barrier is consistent with permeability limitation from the permanent positive charge.",
      "card_ids": ["C108"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L5 gut-wall evidence does not overturn low: quaternary ammonium tropane antimuscarinics can be substrates of uptake transporters (OCTN2 for ipratropium/tiotropium; OCT1/OCT2 for trospium), but trospium oral absorption was not improved by ranitidine inhibition of OCT1/P-gp (C170), and tropane analogs can show high efflux ratios (C195). Ion-pair enhancement for propantheline (C193) is a formulation mechanism, not intrinsic high F.",
